🚀 Day 24 of #DrGViswanathanChallenge

Today, I solved LeetCode 46 – Permutations.

After working with subsets and backtracking, today I explored another important backtracking pattern: generating all permutations.

💡 Approach: Backtracking + Swapping

Instead of creating a separate visited array, I used in-place swapping:

Choose an element for the current position.
Swap it with the element at the current index.
Recursively generate permutations for the next position.
Swap back to restore the original arrangement (backtracking).

For example, with [1,2,3], at index 0 I try 1, 2, and 3 one by one, recursively generating all possible arrangements.

📚 What I Learned:

✅ Generating permutations using recursion
✅ In-place backtracking using swap()
✅ Why we need to restore the array after recursion
✅ Difference between subsets and permutations
✅ Understanding recursion trees more clearly

⏱️ Time Complexity: O(n × n!)
💾 Auxiliary Space: O(n) for the recursion stack (excluding output)

